WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:02.050
Hey Thoughty2 here.

00:00:02.050 --> 00:00:08.370
According to YouTube's analytics roughly 80%
of people watching this video are under 35

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:09.690
years old.

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:15.659
Marketers have invented a name for this demographic
of impressionable young people, they're called

00:00:15.659 --> 00:00:19.240
"Millennials", others have labelled them "Generation
Y".

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:22.850
A millennial is anyone born after 1980.

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:25.380
That includes myself and most of my viewers.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:30.680
In fact Millennials now outnumber every other
generation.

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:38.550
But according to multiple studies we are the
most depressed generation, ever.

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:44.380
Psychology Today reports that the suicide
rate amongst young adults has tripled since

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:50.450
the 1950s and terrifyingly suicide is now
the second most common cause of death amongst

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:52.320
college students.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:59.070
Between 2005 and 2014 the number of clinically
depressed teenagers increased by more than

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:03.570
half a million, three quarter's of whom were
women.

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:09.360
Every year more and more millennials are taking
time off work and are seeking help for mental

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:10.700
health issues.

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:17.750
An even greater number report extremely low
job satisfaction and say they experience difficulty

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:19.810
maintaining relationships.

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:25.560
Young people get a lot of stick, the older
generation have always experienced "juvenoia",

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:31.200
a fear or hostility directed at the younger
generation.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.310
This is nothing new.

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:37.910
Baby boomers, born just after the war, had
a strong work ethic and believed that the

00:01:37.910 --> 00:01:42.840
amount of hours worked should be directly
relational to their income.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:48.140
Baby boomers heavily begrudged the generation
that came after them, generation X.

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:53.970
They were more independent, they changed their
careers more often and believed in a fair

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:55.350
work/life balance.

00:01:55.350 --> 00:02:01.560
And now it has come full circle once again,
Baby Boomers and Generation X-ers alike are

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:05.080
resenting Generation Y, the millennials.

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:11.150
Who have brand new values, such as collaboration,
work that's actually meaningful and impactful,

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:15.650
having fun in the workplace and of course
free food.

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:22.400
Do previous generations look down upon us
just because we're different or is it more

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.010
than that?

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:29.800
Even TIME magazine has poked the millennial
beast, calling them "Lazy, entitled narcissists

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:32.459
who still live with their parents."

00:02:32.459 --> 00:02:33.459
Ouch!

00:02:33.459 --> 00:02:36.610
But unfortunately, they could have a point.

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:41.660
Unlike the generational divides of last century,
there is a lot of research and statistics

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:49.280
that suggest every bad label millennials have
been tarred with, may be well-deserved, even

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:52.440
if it isn't through our own fault.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:54.379
The cold hard facts don't lie.

00:02:54.379 --> 00:02:59.849
The volume of incidents of narcissistic personality
disorder is three times higher for people

00:02:59.849 --> 00:03:02.930
currently in their 20s, than the average.

00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:08.890
Are we really all in love with ourselves because
we received 50 likes on our latest selfie?

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:15.840
Experts believe there are very real, observable
causes of this heightened narcissism.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:20.800
Millennials are the first generation that
were brought up on reality television, and

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:27.790
every year more and more superficial faux-reality
programming is shoved down our throats.

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:33.630
They advertise narcissism above all else,
portraying looks and popularity as the source

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:34.740
of fame and happiness.

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:41.860
In such shows, real human relationships are
often shown as being cheap and disposable.

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:48.599
And then of course there's the social-media
swamp that drowns us in a sea of likes, shares,

00:03:48.599 --> 00:03:49.930
pins and follows.

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:54.960
Countless studies have shown that dopamine,
the pleasure chemical is released in the brain

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:58.510
when we receive likes on our photos and videos.

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:02.120
This system breeds narcissism at its very
core.

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:06.950
It appears the more narcissistic we are and
the more selfies we upload, the more likes

00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:14.060
we will inevitably receive and sadly, this
actually makes up happier.

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:16.940
Experts also accuse us of being entitled.

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:22.639
A recent study revealed that 40% of millennials
believe they should be promoted every two

00:04:22.639 --> 00:04:25.490
years, even if they haven't earnt it.

00:04:25.490 --> 00:04:30.389
Research shows that this entitlement to be
given handouts, regardless of one's performance,

00:04:30.389 --> 00:04:33.449
stems from an unusual place.

00:04:33.449 --> 00:04:35.139
Remember school sports day?

00:04:35.139 --> 00:04:40.180
Even if you came in last place, you still
got a trophy, just for participating.

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:46.919
But even if you didn't as children we were
always rewarded for taking part somehow, "It's

00:04:46.919 --> 00:04:49.979
the taking part that counts", remember.

00:04:49.979 --> 00:04:55.580
Countless studies have proven that this attitude
creates a disingenuous and diluted sense of

00:04:55.580 --> 00:05:01.589
achievement and can be deeply damaging to
our future expectations of what it really

00:05:01.589 --> 00:05:03.889
takes to achieve something.

00:05:03.889 --> 00:05:09.979
This philosophy is unique to our generation,
Generation X-ers, Baby Boomers and those before

00:05:09.979 --> 00:05:14.539
them were all brought up in a deeply competitive
environment.

00:05:14.539 --> 00:05:19.499
It was instilled within them from a young
age that rewards have to be earned through

00:05:19.499 --> 00:05:21.550
hard work and graft.

00:05:21.550 --> 00:05:25.469
They were not told that they were entitled
to anything.

00:05:25.469 --> 00:05:30.930
The so-called silent generation that lived
through both World wars certainly didn't feel

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:31.930
entitled.

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:36.849
They lived through the hardest period of modern
history and everything they had earned they

00:05:36.849 --> 00:05:41.250
had to cling onto with their lives.

00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:45.909
This meant that when they brought up their
children, the Baby Boomers, they instilled

00:05:45.909 --> 00:05:52.749
them with a strict mentality of saving every
penny and an extremely hard work ethic.

00:05:52.749 --> 00:05:57.899
They were told that nothing in life was free
or even expected.

00:05:57.899 --> 00:06:00.389
It was all up to them.

00:06:00.389 --> 00:06:06.110
The baby boomers grew up and had children
of their own, Generation X and thus they passed

00:06:06.110 --> 00:06:08.529
on the same hard work ethic.

00:06:08.529 --> 00:06:16.349
But then a huge shift happened in the global
economy that changed everything.

00:06:16.349 --> 00:06:20.990
Generation X lived through a time of great
prosperity, industry was on the rise and the

00:06:20.990 --> 00:06:26.539
world was finally dragging itself out of the
pit it dug for itself during the war.

00:06:26.539 --> 00:06:29.849
This gave gen-Xers a "can do" attitude.

00:06:29.849 --> 00:06:36.100
And so they taught their children, us millennials,
that if you put your mind to it you can do

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:41.119
anything, become anyone and live a very successful,
happy life.

00:06:41.119 --> 00:06:43.319
You are special.

00:06:43.319 --> 00:06:49.169
This has created a generation of people who
all believe they are special, why wouldn't

00:06:49.169 --> 00:06:50.169
they?

00:06:50.169 --> 00:06:55.709
Their parents have drilled it into their heads
that they are special and unique.

00:06:55.709 --> 00:07:01.069
Millennials are completely fame-obsessed too,
four times as many teenagers would choose

00:07:01.069 --> 00:07:06.789
to be the assistant of a famous person rather
than the CEO of a major corporation.

00:07:06.789 --> 00:07:13.349
In a recent poll, when asked what they would
like to do for a career 54% of 16-year-olds

00:07:13.349 --> 00:07:16.430
answered "Become a celebrity".

00:07:16.430 --> 00:07:20.180
Far gone are the days when becoming a celebrity
was just a side-effect of demonstrating a

00:07:20.180 --> 00:07:23.469
unique talent to the masses.

00:07:23.469 --> 00:07:29.499
Millennials are also lazier, we have grown
up with technology that can think for us.

00:07:29.499 --> 00:07:35.789
And therein lies the biggest problem that's
ruining the lives of young people today.

00:07:35.789 --> 00:07:36.789
Mobile phones.

00:07:36.789 --> 00:07:41.089
I know, you've heard it a hundred times, mobiles
are bad for you.

00:07:41.089 --> 00:07:47.219
But do you really know why, and just how damaging
they are for our mental health?

00:07:47.219 --> 00:07:49.589
Because the research is terrifying.

00:07:49.589 --> 00:07:54.520
So you're out enjoying a meal at a restaurant
with family or friends.

00:07:54.520 --> 00:07:58.479
How many times do you check your phone over
the course of that meal?

00:07:58.479 --> 00:08:04.360
The average millennial checks their phone
over 100 times per day and they touch, tap

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:09.889
or swipe on their phone over 3,000 times per
day.

00:08:09.889 --> 00:08:10.889
Why?

00:08:10.889 --> 00:08:12.860
Because we're all addicts.

00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:19.089
I mentioned earlier that receiving likes releases
dopamine, well so does hundreds of other things

00:08:19.089 --> 00:08:20.759
we do with our phones.

00:08:20.759 --> 00:08:25.419
Taking a picture of your food, taking a selfie,
writing a status update that you just cooked

00:08:25.419 --> 00:08:28.379
the most amazing omelette of your life.

00:08:28.379 --> 00:08:34.479
All of these acts release dopamine, even before
we upload them to the internet.

00:08:34.479 --> 00:08:39.180
Dopamine is released in advance because our
brains anticipate the multitude of likes and

00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:42.099
shares it should hopefully receive.

00:08:42.099 --> 00:08:48.330
Within seconds we start receiving feedback,
this is instant gratification, instant approval

00:08:48.330 --> 00:08:49.500
from our peers.

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:54.519
And it goes on and on, round the clock, we
receive instant gratification when we wake

00:08:54.519 --> 00:08:59.680
up, peer approval before we go to bed and
through the middle of the night.

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:04.790
The dopamine swirls and swirls in our brain
in an ever-lasting continuum of self-fulfilment

00:09:04.790 --> 00:09:06.509
and reward.

00:09:06.509 --> 00:09:10.000
This only serves to perpetuate our narcissism.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:12.540
Until it stops.

00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:17.560
When we upload a photo of our chia seed and
avocado toast and it doesn't receive as much

00:09:17.560 --> 00:09:19.700
attention as we'd hoped.

00:09:19.700 --> 00:09:22.600
In our minds our peers have rejected us.

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:28.110
We are now lost, without purpose, we are now
depressed.

00:09:28.110 --> 00:09:30.550
Does all this sound familiar?

00:09:30.550 --> 00:09:36.970
This cycle of massive releases of dopamine
followed by periods of intense sadness?

00:09:36.970 --> 00:09:41.540
Sounds very much like an alcohol or gambling
addiction doesn't it?

00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:46.310
Wether we like it or not have become a generation
of addicts.

00:09:46.310 --> 00:09:50.529
We check our phone at dinner because we're
addicted, it's the reason we put in on the

00:09:50.529 --> 00:09:52.420
table next to our food.

00:09:52.420 --> 00:09:57.589
Putting the people we should be spending quality
face-to-face time with, in second priority

00:09:57.589 --> 00:10:00.250
to our little dopamine device.

00:10:00.250 --> 00:10:04.730
It's the reason we don't talk to people on
the bus, on the train, at school or work,

00:10:04.730 --> 00:10:08.149
we instead indulge wholly in our addiction
whenever we can.

00:10:08.149 --> 00:10:12.790
Because we are truly addicted, and most of
us don't even realise it.

00:10:12.790 --> 00:10:20.440
A survey of more than 2,000 millennials found
that 93% use their phones in bed and 80% use

00:10:20.440 --> 00:10:22.290
their phones on the toilet.

00:10:22.290 --> 00:10:26.230
But it's not your fault.

00:10:26.230 --> 00:10:29.399
Millennials are simply a product of the environment.

00:10:29.399 --> 00:10:31.730
This is the hand we were dealt.

00:10:31.730 --> 00:10:34.709
We grew up in a time of rapid technological
advancement.

00:10:34.709 --> 00:10:40.940
As long as mobile devices continue to generate
enormous profits, then companies will continue

00:10:40.940 --> 00:10:48.360
to develop new digital mechanisms and social
networks that provide instant gratification.

00:10:48.360 --> 00:10:53.029
And it keeps coming quicker and quicker, we
no longer have to wait for our next fix.

00:10:53.029 --> 00:10:54.660
It's instantaneous.

00:10:54.660 --> 00:10:59.730
We have Snapchat, Facebook, Instagram, Twitter,
Pinterest, WhatsApp any one of which can provide

00:10:59.730 --> 00:11:02.470
our next fix whenever we desire it.

00:11:02.470 --> 00:11:06.949
And if not we can simply connect some digital
candies to make us feel better.

00:11:06.949 --> 00:11:11.310
Our parents didn't have these distractions,
they had to put time and effort into receiving

00:11:11.310 --> 00:11:13.570
the approval of their peers.

00:11:13.570 --> 00:11:19.529
They had to have meaningful conversations
and form strong, deep, and long-lasting relationships

00:11:19.529 --> 00:11:24.069
with both their friends and partners to get
their dopamine fix.

00:11:24.069 --> 00:11:29.720
At work they had to graft day after day and
when that promotion came along, my god had

00:11:29.720 --> 00:11:30.980
they earned it.

00:11:30.980 --> 00:11:36.810
In contrast millennials have become immensely
impatient, we are so use to instant gratification

00:11:36.810 --> 00:11:41.540
that if we don't get a promotion ten minutes
after we start a new job, we get bored of

00:11:41.540 --> 00:11:46.019
it, because it isn't the sort of timeline
we are use to.

00:11:46.019 --> 00:11:51.089
We say we want to make an impact yet we expect
to do so within our first 6 months at a new

00:11:51.089 --> 00:11:52.170
job.

00:11:52.170 --> 00:11:56.540
And when 6 months or a year has gone by and
we're still the tea and coffee guy for the

00:11:56.540 --> 00:12:02.350
office it makes us feel disenfranchised, worthless
and depressed.

00:12:02.350 --> 00:12:07.259
But it's not your fault, you have grown up
in a maelstrom of dopamine on tap whenever

00:12:07.259 --> 00:12:08.769
you desire it.

00:12:08.769 --> 00:12:15.200
So naturally you expect the same instant fulfilment
from your career and relationships.

00:12:15.200 --> 00:12:21.470
But we can't tap a button to help us achieve
that, there is no virtual replacement for

00:12:21.470 --> 00:12:24.890
hard work and genuine conversations.

00:12:24.890 --> 00:12:29.560
We can't swipe right to get promoted and I'm
sorry folks but it's going to take more than

00:12:29.560 --> 00:12:35.769
commenting on your partner's latest selfie
to make them feel genuinely valued and loved.

00:12:35.769 --> 00:12:39.749
Take them on a date for goodness sake and
don't look at your phone the entire time,

00:12:39.749 --> 00:12:43.920
because the moment you do, they are now second
priority.

00:12:43.920 --> 00:12:50.149
So should we be worried about the future,
of a World run by these oh-so-terrible millennials?

00:12:50.149 --> 00:12:54.701
Are millennials really so ill-prepared to
fill the boots of the previous generation

00:12:54.701 --> 00:12:57.170
in 10-20 year's time?

00:12:57.170 --> 00:13:02.709
These narcissistic, self-obsessed, lazy, entitled
young adults who want success given to them

00:13:02.709 --> 00:13:08.740
on a plate, who can't tie down a relationship
or achieve satisfaction in the workplace because

00:13:08.740 --> 00:13:11.600
they're too addicted to social media.

00:13:11.600 --> 00:13:14.350
Well, I wouldn't be so worried.

00:13:14.350 --> 00:13:22.300
Yes, we young people have our issues but we're
actually no different to the last generation,

00:13:22.300 --> 00:13:27.259
Generation X were also labelled as entitled
and lazy when they were young.

00:13:27.259 --> 00:13:33.829
As a society we need to recognise that the
World has completely changed, both technologically

00:13:33.829 --> 00:13:36.860
and culturally over the past twenty years.

00:13:36.860 --> 00:13:42.230
And millennials are the first adopters of
this strange new lifestyle, where you can

00:13:42.230 --> 00:13:46.930
build a billion-dollar business from your
bedroom, if you put your mind to it.

00:13:46.930 --> 00:13:52.790
All our apparent faults are merely symptoms
of trying to adapt to a completely strange,

00:13:52.790 --> 00:13:56.670
new way of life that our parents didn't have
to deal with.

00:13:56.670 --> 00:14:01.790
We face new and unique pressures, stresses
and challenges and we need to find better

00:14:01.790 --> 00:14:06.420
ways to cope with them rather indulging in
social media.

00:14:06.420 --> 00:14:11.560
And for all our faults there are actually
a lot of good things to say about millennials,

00:14:11.560 --> 00:14:14.680
that are rarely brought up.

00:14:14.680 --> 00:14:18.730
Millennials are the most tolerant generation
ever.

00:14:18.730 --> 00:14:23.019
Research shows that prejudice has fallen with
each new generation.

00:14:23.019 --> 00:14:28.459
Millennials are far less racist, homophobic
and sexist than their parents.

00:14:28.459 --> 00:14:34.630
Pew research asked adults aged between 18-29
what their most important priority was during

00:14:34.630 --> 00:14:35.630
their lifetime.

00:14:35.630 --> 00:14:39.100
52% answered "Being a good parent".

00:14:39.100 --> 00:14:42.819
30% said "Have a successful marriage".

00:14:42.819 --> 00:14:46.910
21% replied "Helping others in need".

00:14:46.910 --> 00:14:51.860
Now that doesn't sound like a generation that
is completely self-absorbed now, does it?

00:14:51.860 --> 00:14:58.370
And our inability to settle into a normal
9-5 job may not be entirely due to our reliance

00:14:58.370 --> 00:14:59.980
on social media.

00:14:59.980 --> 00:15:05.610
Maybe it's because we all want more than the
monotonous normality of working for some heartless

00:15:05.610 --> 00:15:07.610
corporate giant.

00:15:07.610 --> 00:15:12.069
Research shows that millennials are the most
entrepreneurial generation ever.

00:15:12.069 --> 00:15:17.130
Spurred on by young do-it-yourself success
stories like Elon Musk and Mark Zuckerberg.

00:15:17.130 --> 00:15:24.040
As a generation we have an unstoppable appetite
to achieve something big and make an impact.

00:15:24.040 --> 00:15:27.740
Right after we've finished sending this hilarious
Snapchat.

00:15:27.740 --> 00:15:32.619
This mentality might be proving difficult
for large corporations who need to employ

00:15:32.619 --> 00:15:37.889
cheap young labor that can sit in an office
and push buttons for eight hours a day.

00:15:37.889 --> 00:15:43.170
But it's changing the world in ways our parents
could have never imagined.

00:15:43.170 --> 00:15:47.619
There's no doubt we need to spend more time
interacting with one another and less time

00:15:47.619 --> 00:15:53.790
on social media, all the research has shown
that it does bad things for our mental health.

00:15:53.790 --> 00:15:58.970
But we need to accept that the world is rapidly
changing.

00:15:58.970 --> 00:16:04.769
Millennials have their faults but they are
also contributing to the world in so many

00:16:04.769 --> 00:16:06.839
incredible ways.

00:16:06.839 --> 00:16:12.019
Just like the generation before us, and the
generation before them.

00:16:12.019 --> 00:16:18.559
Change has always come and always will, Its
inevitable, so we need to find a way to work

00:16:18.559 --> 00:16:26.560
with it for the better instead of spending
our time pointing out the faults in others.

00:16:26.560 --> 00:16:36.720
Thanks for watching.

